"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes how to deploy \c{xle}, IW4x Xbox Live social
services server, and how to keep its database current across \c{xle}
upgrades. For the command line interface run \c{xle\ --help}.

\h1#intro|Introduction|

The game uses the Xbox Live social services to find the players it shows in
its friends list and to tell them about each other's games. \c{xle} keeps
the state of these services, starting with the social relationships, in a
PostgreSQL database.

The current version of \c{xle} prepares this database: with \c{--migrate} it
creates the database schema or migrates it to the current version and exits.
The services themselves arrive in the later versions, and this document
describes their deployment as they do.

The examples in this document assume a Fedora or Debian-based Linux system
and use the following locations:

\
/opt/xle/                       installation
/etc/xle/xle.options            options file
/var/lib/xle/                   home of the xle user
\

You can use other locations, as long as you adjust the commands accordingly.

\h1#install|Installation|

Before we begin, make sure the following is available on the host:

\ul|

\li|GCC 16 or later, since \c{xle} is written in C++26.|

\li|The \c{build2} toolchain 0.18.0 or later (see the
\l{https://build2.org/install.xhtml Installation Instructions}).|

\li|An account with \c{sudo} access.||

The remaining dependencies are \c{build2} packages and are built from source
along with \c{xle}.

We also need a copy of the \c{xle} repository for its setup script:

\
$ git clone https://github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/xle.git
\

\dl|

\li|\b{1. Set Up PostgreSQL}\n

The \c{xle/etc/private/postgresql/xle-postgresql-setup} script installs and
starts the PostgreSQL server from the distribution's packages and creates the
\c{xle} database owned by the \c{xle} role:

\
$ xle/xle/etc/private/postgresql/xle-postgresql-setup
set up PostgreSQL database 'xle' owned by role 'xle'? [y/n]: y
+ sudo dnf install --assumeyes postgresql-server
...
+ psql: CREATE ROLE xle LOGIN
+ psql: CREATE DATABASE xle OWNER xle TEMPLATE template0 ...
database 'xle' is ready
  info: run 'xle --db-name xle --migrate' as user 'xle' to create
  its schema
\

The \c{xle} role authenticates with the \c{peer} method, which is the
PostgreSQL default for the local UNIX-domain socket: the system user that
runs \c{xle} has the name of the role and needs no password.

The script skips the steps that are already done, so it is safe to run it
again. It can also print its plan without doing anything (\c{--print-only})
and use other names (\c{--database} and \c{--role}). See the comments at the
beginning of the script for details.

\N|On other distributions we install and start the PostgreSQL server with the
distribution's tools and then execute the following statements as the
PostgreSQL superuser:

\
CREATE ROLE xle LOGIN;
CREATE DATABASE xle OWNER xle TEMPLATE template0 ENCODING 'UTF8';
\

|

|

\li|\n\b{2. Build and Install}\n

We build \c{xle} in a \c{bpkg} build configuration. You will want to keep
this configuration around since it is also what we upgrade \c{xle} with (see
\l{#upgrade Upgrading}). In this guide we use \c{~/xle-build/} as the
configuration directory:

\
$ bpkg create -d ~/xle-build cc     \
  config.cxx=g++                    \
  config.cc.coptions=-O2            \
  config.bin.rpath=/opt/xle/lib     \
  config.install.root=/opt/xle      \
  config.install.sudo=sudo

$ cd ~/xle-build
$ bpkg build xle@https://github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/xle.git#main
$ bpkg install --all --recursive
\

The \c{config.bin.rpath} value embeds the library directory into the
executable so that it finds the shared libraries installed next to it, and
\c{--recursive} installs these libraries along with \c{xle}. Let's make sure
the result runs:

\
$ /opt/xle/bin/xle --version
xle 0.1.0
libxle 0.1.0
...
\

|

\li|\n\b{3. Create System User}\n

\c{xle} runs as an unprivileged system user with the name of its database
role:

\
$ sudo useradd --system --user-group --create-home \
  --home-dir /var/lib/xle --shell /usr/sbin/nologin xle
\

|

\li|\n\b{4. Write Options File}\n

\c{xle} reads its options from the file that we pass with
\c{--options-file}. Each option is on its own line, optionally followed by a
space or an equal sign and the value, and the lines starting with \c{#} are
comments. Let's create \c{/etc/xle/xle.options} with the following contents:

\
# The xle options (see xle --help for details).
#
--db-name xle
\

The file is readable by \c{root} and the \c{xle} group only, since it holds
the database password once the database is on another host (see
\l{#operation-remote Remote Database}):

\
$ sudo chown root:xle /etc/xle/xle.options
$ sudo chmod 640 /etc/xle/xle.options
\

|

\li|\n\b{5. Create Database Schema}\n

\c{xle} creates its database schema when run with \c{--migrate}, which we do
as the \c{xle} user:

\
$ sudo -u xle /opt/xle/bin/xle \
  --options-file /etc/xle/xle.options --migrate -v
created database xle schema version 1
\

The same command migrates the schema after an upgrade (see
\l{#upgrade-schema Migrating Database Schema}).||

\h1#operation|Operation|

\h#operation-diagnostics|Diagnostics|

\c{xle} writes its diagnostics to \c{stderr}. The \c{--verbose} option sets
the verbosity from 0 to 3. Level 0 prints the errors, and each next level
adds a kind of message: warnings at 1 (the default), informational messages
at 2, and trace messages at 3. The \c{-q}, \c{-v}, and \c{-V} options are the
shortcuts for levels 0, 2, and 3.

An error names the database and the reason that PostgreSQL gives. For
example, this is what we get while the server is down:

\
error: unable to migrate database xle: database error: connection to
server on socket \"/run/postgresql/.s.PGSQL.5432\" failed: No such
file or directory
\

\h#operation-database|Database|

\c{xle} opens up to \c{--db-max-connections} connections (5 by default) and
performs the database operations on as many threads. Every operation runs in
a serializable transaction. Together with the other clients of the server,
the connections have to fit into the server's \c{max_connections} (100 by
default).

PostgreSQL vacuums and analyzes the tables on its own (autovacuum is enabled
by default), so the database needs no regular maintenance besides the backups.

\h#operation-backup|Backups|

The \c{xle} role owns the database and so can dump it. We use the custom
format, which is compressed and which \c{pg_restore} can restore selectively:

\
$ sudo -u xle pg_dump --format=custom \
  --file=/var/lib/xle/xle-$(date +%F).dump xle
\

To make a backup every night, we can add a line to the \c{xle} user's
\c{crontab} (\c{sudo\ crontab\ -u\ xle\ -e}), escaping the percent sign as
\c{cron} requires:

\
0 4 * * * pg_dump -Fc -f /var/lib/xle/xle-$(date +\%F).dump xle
\

\N|These backups are on the same host as the database, so make sure they are
also copied to another one.|

To restore a backup, we drop the database, let the setup script create an
empty one, and restore the backup into it:

\
$ sudo -u postgres dropdb xle
$ xle/xle/etc/private/postgresql/xle-postgresql-setup --yes
$ sudo -u xle pg_restore --dbname=xle \
  /var/lib/xle/xle-2026-10-09.dump
\

The backup includes the schema version. If an older \c{xle} made it, then we
migrate the restored schema (see \l{#upgrade-schema Migrating Database
Schema}).

\h#operation-remote|Remote Database|

The setup script configures a local server. To use a server on another host,
we give the role a password and allow it to connect over the network:

\
ALTER ROLE xle PASSWORD '...';
\

The server's \c{pg_hba.conf} then needs a line like this one, with the
network of the \c{xle} host:

\
host  xle  xle  192.0.2.0/24  scram-sha-256
\

And the options file names the server and the credentials:

\
--db-host     db.example.org
--db-port     5432
--db-user     xle
--db-password ...
\

\N|The command line of a process is visible to the other users of the host.
The password belongs in the options file, which only \c{root} and the
\c{xle} group can read, or in the PostgreSQL password file (see
\c{PGPASSFILE} in the PostgreSQL documentation).|

\h1#upgrade|Upgrading|

At this point we assume that \c{xle} is installed as described in
\l{#install Installation} and that the \c{bpkg} configuration from step 2 is
in \c{~/xle-build/}.

We first fetch the new version and see what we are upgrading to:

\
$ cd ~/xle-build
$ bpkg fetch
$ bpkg status xle
!xle configured 0.1.0 available 0.2.0
\

If the new version changes the database schema, then the upgrade also
migrates it, and we want a fresh backup before that (see
\l{#operation-backup Backups}).

We uninstall the old version before installing the new one, so that the
files the new version no longer has don't remain installed:

\
$ bpkg uninstall --all --recursive
$ bpkg build --upgrade --recursive xle
$ bpkg install --all --recursive
$ sudo -u xle /opt/xle/bin/xle \
  --options-file /etc/xle/xle.options --migrate -v
migrated database xle schema from version 1 to 2
\

\N|The \c{--recursive} option makes \c{bpkg} upgrade and install the
dependencies of \c{xle} as well. See
\l{https://build2.org/bpkg/doc/bpkg-pkg-build.xhtml \c{bpkg-pkg-build(1)}}
for details.|

\h#upgrade-schema|Migrating Database Schema|

The database records the version of its schema and each \c{xle} version uses
one schema version. \c{xle} can migrate the schemas from a range of earlier
versions to its own. With \c{--migrate} it does what the database needs and
with \c{-v} it reports what it did:

\
created database xle schema version 2
migrated database xle schema from version 1 to 2
database xle schema version 2 is current
\

The migration runs in a single transaction, so a failed migration leaves the
database as it was. If several hosts run \c{--migrate} at once, then the
migrations happen one after another and all but the first find the schema
current.

\c{xle} fails without changing anything if the schema is outside its range:

\
error: unable to migrate database xle: database schema version 1
is too old to migrate
error: unable to migrate database xle: database schema version 3
is newer than 2
\

An old schema is migrated in steps, first with an intermediate \c{xle}
version that still supports it. The release notes (the \c{NEWS} file) say
when a version drops the support for a schema. A newer schema means the
database belongs to a newer \c{xle}. To go back to an earlier \c{xle}, we
reinstall it and restore the backup made before the migration, which loses
the changes made since.

\h1#develop|Development|

This chapter is for the \c{xle} developers and assumes the development setup
from the repository's \c{README.md}.

\h#develop-test|Testing with PostgreSQL|

The \c{libxle} tests use the in-memory stores unless we give them a
PostgreSQL maintenance database (normally \c{postgres}) on a server where our
user may create databases. The setup script grants this permission to the
user we name with \c{--test-role}:

\
$ xle/etc/private/postgresql/xle-postgresql-setup --test-role \"$USER\"
$ bdep test '!config.libxle.test.pgsql=postgres'
\

Each test creates its own database, named \c{xle_test_} followed by a random
suffix, and drops it once done. The tests connect with the \c{libpq}
defaults, so the \c{PGHOST} and \c{PGPORT} environment variables select
another server.

\h#develop-schema|Changing Database Schema|

The persistent classes are in \c{libxle/libxle/model.hxx}. The development
build (\c{config.libxle.develop=true}) translates this header with the ODB
compiler and copies the generated code to \c{libxle/libxle/pregenerated/},
where the consumption build takes it from. So after changing the header we
build in a development configuration and commit the regenerated code along
with the header. The \c{model} test fails if the two are out of sync.

The ODB compiler registers the schema under the name \c{xle}
(\c{--schema-name} in \c{libxle/libxle/buildfile}), which has to match the
name that \c{libxle/libxle/pgsql.cxx} creates and migrates. The schema
catalog of a process holds the schemas of all its libraries, so the name
keeps the tables of the linked \c{libobe} out of the \c{xle} database.

The schema versions are defined in \c{model.hxx}:

\
#define LIBXLE_SCHEMA_VERSION_BASE 1
#define LIBXLE_SCHEMA_VERSION      1, open
\

The current version is \i{open} while its changes are unreleased, and the
changes made in the meantime fold into it. The ODB compiler records the closed
versions in the changelog, \c{libxle/libxle/model.xml}, and generates the
migration statements from it. The changelog is both its input and its output,
so we commit it as well. A schema version goes through the following steps:

\dl|

\li|\b{1. Close Version}\n

Before releasing an \c{xle} version with schema changes, we remove \c{open}
from \c{LIBXLE_SCHEMA_VERSION}, build, and commit the updated changelog.|

\li|\n\b{2. Open Next Version}\n

After the release, we bump the current version (for example, to
\c{2,\ open}) for the changes that follow.|

\li|\n\b{3. Raise Base Version}\n

To drop the migration from the oldest versions, we raise
\c{LIBXLE_SCHEMA_VERSION_BASE}. The deployments with an older schema then
migrate in steps (see \l{#upgrade-schema Migrating Database Schema}), so the
release notes have to say so.||

See the Database Schema Evolution chapter of the ODB manual for the changes
that the migration supports.
"
